heonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-26T10:38:22-08:00","id":58172,"title":"","content":"Nicely written! I like the idea, the organic way in which the history of the cosmology is woven into other stories, and the story potential it offers. Might snag it as an antagonist or ally for the next game I run.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Once is happenstance. Twice is coincidence. Three times is enemy action. Four times is Infrastructure.<br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://img513.imageshack.us/img513/4478/8vno.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /><br /> Rule #2: Never let the game-mechanics get in the way of a good story.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1642-paddon","name":"Paddon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1642_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"326"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-26T13:38:07-08:00","id":58286,"title":"","content":"Well done. After the Network Zero presented as a Compact on the rise to become a Conspiracy, it is good to see a full fleshed dying hunter organisation (I don't count the short pages about the Roman Aves Minerva in a Requiem SAS). So far the only other example is Ninestar's &quot;Obsidian Brotherhood&quot; (although the reason for its decline is quite different).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/8.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/305-kergonan","name":"Kergonan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar305_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"35"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-01-28T04:14:13-08:00","id":59689,"title":"","content":"thanks for both of you, and I'm happy you like it <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> and Kergonan- yeah, that was part of my thinking process: making a &quot;mirror&quot; for Network 0's rise. While I'm also aware of the Obsidian Brotherhood, I prefer the idea of a conspiracy dying out of lack of members than of losing its endowment- especially that the former can be reversed, and the latter is based around the assumption that a conspiracy without an endowment is &quot;dead&quot; :P","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T15:17:02-07:00","id":116697,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> 000- After your time within the Fangs, you learn enough from the remaining ancient texts in order to improve your performance when fighting against the dead. You get +3 die bonus for when Summoning, Exorcising, Abjuring, Warding or Binding a ghost or a similar entity from the underworld. </div> </div> </div>Do they have access to Low Magic?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2411-raistlin","name":"Raistlin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"713"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-20T15:49:35-07:00","id":116704,"title":"","content":"that's open for the ST. In general, their supernatural knowledge is expressed in their Endowments and bonus for effecting ghosts, which they do like any other mortals under the rules of the God Machine Chronicles (a.k.a, summoning, abjuring, etc)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-05-15T11:37:38-07:00","id":138276,"title":"","content":"so now that Mortal Remains is out, I was thinking if I checked the Sin Eater section to see if there is something that I feel to be updated- and surprisingly, I found that the Conspiracy works perfectly with the &quot;ghostwalkers'&quot; history! The possibility of Inanna as a Bound? Check. The low numbers and knowledge about Sin Eaters during the Medial Ages (which is when the Ebony Fangs were the their highest)? Check. The rise in the recordings of Ghostwalkers during the WWs, which is when the Conspiracy fell from grace? Check and check. In short, I think they work really good with what was given in the chapter :P","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-03-02T00:30:50-08:00","id":840463,"title":"","content":"<b><u>Hunters of the First Turn: Devoured With Obsidian Teeth</u></b><br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Those people of Kemet (both mortal and not) who dare to venture outside of their lands may find a strange sight - groups of nomadic people, wearing all black who travel the roads in perfect silence. They never look others in the eye, and never speak unless asked for. They carry warning signs on their clothes and skin in many different languages, both known and not. A few of the Arisen even swear they saw some signs in not-so-corrupted Iremite. Whatever the language, all are used to say the same things: curse, plague, fear,</span><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i> death</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Most people stay away from these wanderers, listening to the warnings and rumors which surround them. For they are the Su, the nomadic exorcists, the outcasts among outcasts, those who devour the dead with Obsidian Teeth in the name of their goddess.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Both the Undead and the Deathless stay away from their path, for they do not fear death. They are already cursed, exiled, and left to roam the lands. And they recruit only from those who are seen as damned by the rest. They offer their help to anyone who wishes to deal with the unquiet dead, especially when they have taken loved ones as their thralls. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Usually, the Su hunt down ghosts and similar shades, yet they seem to hold a special grudge to the &quot;Children of Inanna&quot;, following them whenever they go. They name their greatest enemy as the Gidim, using the word not to describe the Hungry Dead of Kemet (they use the word Gallu for all of them) but a special type of shade which offers a second chance to the dying. Such Bargains endanger the boundary between the world of the living and Irkalla, the world of the dead.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">While they were once a rare sight in Kemet, lately more and more of those silent pilgrims show up in the Black Land. They seem to have noticed the changes taking place, and that the veil between worlds has become thinner. The dead return to life, and the living may die. In response, they come to investigate if someone, or </span><span style=\"font-family:Arial\"><i>something</i></span><span style=\"font-family:Arial\">, is emerging from the mouth of the Underworld. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">As the Su disperse throughout the Black Land, they both target and mingle with the local Scorpion Cults, collecting information about the new forms of restless dead rising from the sands. They also recruit, offering freedom for escaped slaves, unwanted children and exiled betrayers. Through it all, they are quite adaptable, calling themselves the Kef-Nhdut, the Kemetic translation of their name.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">While the Arisen are more powerful than the Su, they can't shake the feeling that something is wrong with them. They don't hold secrets, freely saying their history to any cultist who may ask. And those stories ring a bell. They tell a story about a mighty city ruled by a necromancer, a queen both a lady and a beast. They tell a story about how the city fell into the world of the dead, leaving them to find their way out under an oath to their queen to never stop hunting the dead. When asked when it happened, they say they don't know. After all, who can tell how time flows in the Underworld, in a place without a Sun or a Moon? Their ancestors could have traveled that labyrinth of rivers for hundreds of years and they won't be able to tell.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The Arisen who hear about those stories can't deny the parallels between them and their own history. Some wonder if perhaps the fall of Kigal is a corrupted version of Irem's destruction, and that Ereshkigal was how those people remembered the Shani\u2019atu, making them into the fallen aristocrats of the Ki-En-Gir. Other dare to say even more extreme theories. That perhaps Ereshkigal was an exiled and forsaken Shani\u2019atu. Or a mortal sorceress inspired by them, who did her own experiment far in the Land Between Rivers. </span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">Still, the most disturbing thing is not how the Su explain themselves, but how they explain the Arisen. After a long discussion between themselves, they decided that the Deathless are no other than the incarnations of the Anna, the Judges of Irkalla who serve their goddess. Worse is the way they translate their own mythology to the Kemetic lexicon in order to make recruitment easier- when they named their goddess, they didn't called her after the powerful Eset or the gentle Nebthet. No, their goddess is fierce, their goddess is loyal, their goddess judge the dead and offer no second chances to those who escape her grip.</span><br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:Arial\">The goddess is Ammut, and she would feast upon the hearts of her enemies with obsidian fangs.<br /> <br /> <b><u>Su's Expanded Endowment- Words of Irkalla</u></b></span><br /> The Su know the language of the Dead, and are given the Mantle of the Underworld by their dread goddess. Using gifts meant only to the lords of the Great Bellow and the guardians of the words of the Underworld, they can make Laws, bind Oaths and invoke Names in order to influence both the living and the dead. It is their right as the Messengers of Irkalla to do so, and the emissaries of the Underworld make sure only they would know those secrets.<br /> <br /> The Words of Irkalla are divided into 3 different merits, each goes 1-5. The first is called the Laws, which allow them to act as Ereshkigal's representatives in the world of the living and use her name to use the Old Laws on both worlds. In order to invoke the law, the Fang must first to phrase it. the Law must contain a number of factors- first, calling one of the names of the goddess, defining the target among the &quot;living&quot; and the &quot;dead&quot; and name what it is meant to do. Beings like spirits and angels could be targeted, but must be named as &quot;gods&quot;. After phrasing the Law, it must be be engraved into a physical item, and so it must be adjusted to the use of that item. That means that that if you want to stop an undead creature from, entering into your house, you can engrave a Law on a key which means &quot;By the name of Ereshkigal, the Dead won't be able to Enter to the house which was locked by this key&quot;.<br /> <br /> After engraving the Law into the item, the character must than make the following Roll to see if she successfully used her Mandate and didn't offended Ereshkigal. If everything goes right, the Law is ready to be used. If things go wrong, however, it could draw the wrath of the goddess, and she won't give her gifts generously..<br /> <b>Cost:</b> One Willpower point<br /> <b>Action:</b> Instant<br /> <b>Dice Pool:</b> Occult + laws of Irkalla- [every use of the Laws during that day. reset at midnight]<br /> <b>Roll Results:</b><br /> <b>Dramatic Failure:</b> Apparently, you called down the wrong Law or misused your Mandate over the Dead, and your goddess decided to show her displeasure from you. You suffer 1 level of Bashing damage for every dot you have in the Laws, plus another level for every time you used the ritual that day.<br /> <b>Failure:</b> The Laws is wrong, and it won't work- or that for some reason Ereshkigal decided to not lend you her help. However, you don't know it- and you won't know until you'll try.<br /> <b>Success:</b> The Laws works exactly as you wrote it down. It works for [Dots in Laws]*2 hours. Also, next time you try to use exactly that Law, add +1 die up to their dots in the Endowment.<br /> <b>Exceptional Success</b>: same as regular Success, but you get +2 die for the next time you use that Law.<br /> <br /> Laws do not need to be re-engraved before every use.<br /> <br /> The second use are the Oaths, who use Ereshkigal's authority as the judge of the dead, while the hunter simply use as a medium and represent her among the living.In order to bind an Oath, the parties who take part in it must stand before the Messenger of the Underworld and proclaim each what they commit to do, and what will happen if they break their word, and finally all sides swear upon Ereshkigal's name. The agreement does not need to be written, for Irkalla is watching and its Old Laws would enforce the pact. Then, each person who takes a part in the Oath (including the hunter) must expand a single WP point. Once the act is done, the Oath is valid- no roll needed. The dots in the Oath represent the number of individuals the Messenger is allowed to bind in a single pact- first dot Oaths may only bind the actual people who take part in the ceremony. 3 dots may include all future members of certain group or family. 5 dots Oath may including all future members of such group, living or dead.<br /> <br /> Such Oaths may also by taken between a person and himself, in which case Ereshkigal is seen as the one to be offended if the Oath is broken- which may have some unwanted consequences. Oaths do no end with the death of the participant unless noted to do so, and such Oaths may force the person to leave a ghost behind, and the people who take part in the ceremony may not need to use their true name or identity, as long as they are truly connected to the group they represent.<br /> <br /> The problem comes when you need to nullify the Oath- in order to even try and do so, the hunter must have Status: Ebony Fangs higher than the one to bind the Oath. Then, she must roll her own Oaths against the binder's Oaths. Dramatic Failure would make her suffer 1 dot of Lethal damage equal to her dots in the Endowment. Exceptional Success would also give the hunter another WP point. A Messenger may also nullify an Oath if someone who belong to the group represented by the one to take part in the ceremony yet has higher Status wish to terminate the agreement. Such Status requirements are to be compared not to the current ones, but to the ones the participants had during the ceremony.<br /> <br /> Finally, the last use are the Names of Irkalla- by knowing the name of a person (which needs to be the name given in &quot;birth&quot;, including Ephemeral beings) the Fang may force either a Condition upon her or terminate such a Condition. Doing so require expanding a WP point and a roll of the Names+Occult verse the victim's Resolve+Composure+Supernatural Tolerance. The target must be present before the Messenger and be able to sense her somehow.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <b>(for more information about vampires, hunters and more in the First Turn, see the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/828132-open-invite-age-of-azar\">Age of Azar</a>)</b>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","user":"LostLight","date":". 03-02-2016, 03:43 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}